{"pr":193,"result":{"status":"fulfilled","value":{"chunk_id":"cff819","wall_time_seconds":0.582148875,"exit_code":0,"original_token_count":1519,"output":"{\"baseRefOid\":\"b0654691af924cacbc1bcd6f2ccc6dc84272bfd9\",\"body\":\"`release`, `release artifact-manifest` and `init` granted their `ApplyPermit` from a literal `false`. The permit's own rule is that the dry-run decision comes from the parsed flag, read once near the top of a handler; a literal re-derives it and can silently disagree with `--dry-run` if the surrounding branch ever changes.\\n\\nI checked each site before changing it. All three are already safe by control flow: `release` returns its preview before reaching the permit, `artifact-manifest` rejects `--dry-run` up front (`ReleaseArtifactManifestDryRun`), and `init` takes the no-write branch under `--dry-run`. So this changes no behavior and no CLI contract, and needs no spec change or changeset.\\n\\nEach site now calls `ApplyPermit::granted_unless_dry_run(global.dry_run)`. A new architecture test, `apply_permits_are_never_granted_from_a_literal`, fails on any `true` or `false` argument in production code outside `permit.rs`, including a reformatted multi-line call with a trailing comma. I confirmed it fails when one literal is put back. The other three `ApplyPermit` sites in the codebase already used the flag.\\n\\nThe CLI crate suite passes (402 tests).\\n\\nStacked on #190 because both change `architecture.rs`.\",\"commits\":[{\"authoredDate\":\"2026-10-14T01:58:25Z\",\"authors\":[{\"email\":\"gabe@techworx.dev\",\"id\":\"MDQ6VXNlcjExNjc0Nw==\",\"login\":\"castrog\",\"name\":\"Gabe Castro\"}],\"committedDate\":\"2026-10-14T02:31:30Z\",\"messageBody\":\"…nforce it\\n\\nrelease, release artifact-manifest and init granted their permit from a literal false. Each was already safe by control flow, but a literal can silently disagree with --dry-run if the surrounding branch changes. They now read global.dry_run, and an architecture test rejects a litera